Three‑way quicksort, introduced by Dijkstra, partitions the array into three regions: elements less than the pivot, elements equal to the pivot, and elements greater than the pivot. This reduces the amount of work when many elements are equal because the equal region is left untouched in further recursive calls. A concise implementation in Java looks like:
```java
int lt = lo, i = lo, gt = hi;
while (i <= gt) {
    if (arr[i] < pivot) swap(arr, lt++, i++);
    else if (arr[i] > pivot) swap(arr, i, gt--);
    else i++;
}
// recurse on [lo, lt-1] and [gt+1, hi]
```
The algorithm still runs in O(n log n) on average but can approach O(n) when all keys are identical.

