A generator stops when it reaches the end of the function or encounters a `return` statement without a value. If you `return` inside the loop, the generator finishes immediately after the first `yield`. To keep producing values, let the loop run to the next `yield` and avoid returning until you really want to end, for example: `def squares(n):
    for i in range(n):
        yield i*i` will produce `n` values before finishing.

If you nest a dataclass inside another and both are defined with the same name in different scopes, the default __repr__ includes the fully qualified name, which can look like duplication. For example, `@dataclass
class Outer:
    inner: 'Inner'

    @dataclass
    class Inner:
        value: int` will print `Outer(inner=Outer.Inner(value=1))`. This is expected behavior; you can customize __repr__ with `repr=False` on fields or define your own __repr__ method.

