The @property decorator turns a method into a read‑only attribute, so you can compute a value on the fly while letting callers use dot‑notation like obj.value. It lets you add logic (validation, caching, lazy calculation) without changing the public API. For example:

class Circle:
    def __init__(self, r):
        self._radius = r
    @property
    def radius(self):
        return self._radius
    @property
    def area(self):
        return 3.14159 * self._radius ** 2

Now c.area works like an attribute but runs code each time. Use it when you need a computed attribute or want to hide internal state.

