KMP preprocesses the pattern to build a "prefix function" (also called failure function) that tells you the length of the longest proper prefix which is also a suffix for each prefix of the pattern. When a mismatch occurs, you shift the pattern by the amount indicated by this function instead of moving back to the start, so you never re‑examine characters that you already know match. The preprocessing runs in O(m) time for a pattern of length m, and the search runs in O(n) time for a text of length n. For example, in Python you can compute the table with:
```python
def prefix_function(p):
    pi = [0]*len(p)
    j = 0
    for i in range(1, len(p)):
        while j and p[i] != p[j]:
            j = pi[j-1]
        if p[i] == p[j]:
            j += 1
        pi[i] = j
    return pi
```

