To insert a key, start at the root and compare the key with the current node’s value. If the key is smaller, move to the left child; if larger, move to the right child. Repeat this until you reach a null pointer, then create a new node there. The algorithm preserves the BST property because you only go left for smaller values and right for larger ones. In code it looks like:

Node* insert(Node* root, int key) {
    if (!root) return new Node(key);
    if (key < root->val) root->left = insert(root->left, key);
    else if (key > root->val) root->right = insert(root->right, key);
    return root;
}

